-### Variant: LS-Gaussian (Sparse Rendering & Accelerator)
-Optimizes for real-time rendering on edge devices (e.g., Jetson AGX Orin) by leveraging inter-frame continuity [S9].
-
-**Algorithm:**
-1.  **Viewpoint Transformation:** Instead of rendering each frame separately, transform the viewpoint between consecutive frames. Only one frame in every 6 frames is fully rendered [S9].
-2.  **Workload Prediction:** Predict the workload for each tile based on viewpoint transformation to enable balanced parallel computation [S9].
-3.  **Accelerator Architecture:** Co-design a hardware architecture with a streaming pipeline that allows continuous processing across hardware modules without global synchronization [S9].
-
-**Pitfall:** Rendering quality degrades noticeably as the number of consecutively transformed frames increases due to the accumulation of interpolation errors [S9].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Continuous Attenuation Model for 3D Gaussian Splatting](9624-continuous-attenuation-model-for-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)
-Reformulates rasterization into a volumetric ray-marching framework by mapping Gaussians to a continuous attenuation field $\sigma(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{w})$. This ensures transmittance isosurfaces remain unbiased and ellipsoidal, preventing surface erosion and multi-view inconsistency seen in prior approximations like GGGS [9624-continuous-attenuation-model-for-3d-gaussian-splatting.md].
+### Corrected Scene-Extent
+A method to calculate scene extent based on the SfM point cloud rather than camera positions to avoid bias from camera distribution (e.g., orbiting a small object in a large environment) [S3]. It sets itself apart from the default 3DGS extent calculation by using the spatial distribution of the reconstructed points, ensuring the scene volume is accurately represented regardless of camera placement [S3]. Choose this when camera poses are clustered or do not encompass the full scene volume [S3].
 
-### [Sorting and Rendering Pipeline Optimization](12180-sorting-and-rendering-pipeline-optimization.md)
-Addresses computational bottlenecks in sorting and attribute fetching. It maps software strategies to hardware constraints (e.g., Roofline model) or replaces standard sorting with hardware-friendly, comparison-free logic and sorting-free approximations [12180-sorting-and-rendering-pipeline-optimization.md].
+### Selective Defocus Blurring
+Predicts a per-Gaussian scale offset $\delta s_j$ to selectively enlarge covariances, creating pixel-wise variable blur kernels that match spatially varying scene blurriness [S4]. It sets itself apart from naive Gaussian blur (which applies a single kernel to the whole image) and learnable Gaussian kernels (which optimize a single mean/variance) by allowing spatially varying blur shapes and magnitudes [S4]. Choose this when training on images with defocus blur or motion blur that varies across the scene [S4].
 
-### [LangSplat Training Procedure](12438-langsplat-training-procedure.md)
-Extends the standard pipeline to incorporate semantic language features (e.g., CLIP) alongside photometric rendering. It disables densification and pruning during language-aware training to maintain semantic consistency [12438-langsplat-training-procedure.md].
+### Denoise-GS
+A self-supervised denoising method for sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting that combines unsupervised image denoising with explicitly differentiable 3DGS [S5]. It sets itself apart from supervised methods (which require clean annotations) and Noise2Noise (which requires paired noisy images) by using Noise2Void (N2V), which requires no paired data [S5]. Choose this for sparse-view scenarios with noisy images where paired clean data is unavailable [S5].
 
-### [Gaussian Representation and Truncation](12855-gaussian-representation-and-truncation.md)
-Modifies the definition, spatial extent, or lifecycle of 3D Gaussians. Includes alternative kernel shapes, opacity-independent culling, and voxel-based constraints to improve fidelity and efficiency [12855-gaussian-representation-and-truncation.md].
+### Backscatter Estimation and Optimization
+Integrates ocean optics models into 3DGS by learning direct attenuation ($B_d$), infinite backscatter ($B_\infty$), and backscatter coefficient ($B_b$) alongside scene geometry [S6]. It sets itself apart from vanilla 3DGS by accounting for underwater scattering effects [S6]. Choose this for underwater scene reconstruction [S6]. A key pitfall is that the camera sensor must have a linear response to light; in-camera processed .jpg images cannot be used unless non-linearities are reversed [S6].
 
-### [DeSplat](12925-desplat.md)
-Decomposes the scene into static scene Gaussians ($G_s$) and per-view distractor Gaussians ($G_d$) to handle transient occluders (e.g., pedestrians) without external semantic models [12925-desplat.md].
+### Multi-Scale Constraint
+A technique using position-aware point adaptive control to apply different scales to Gaussians based on viewer distance, improving rendering of distant objects [S7]. It sets itself apart from standard densification by scaling the cloning/splitting thresholds based on the Gaussian's distance from the camera center [S7]. Choose this for large-scale scenes with varying distances [S7].
 
-### [360 Gaussian Splatting](12929-360-gaussian-splatting.md)
-Modifies the differentiable rasterizer to support spherical rendering and uses OpenSfM (which supports equirectangular inputs) to process 360-degree camera images directly [12929-360-gaussian-splatting.md].
+### QuickSplat Optimization Process
+An optimization framework that uses learned networks for initialization, densification, and parameter updates to accelerate surface reconstruction [S8]. It sets itself apart from iterative gradient-based densification by using neural networks ($f_I, f_D, f_O$) to predict Gaussian updates directly [S8]. Choose this for fast surface reconstruction where training time is a bottleneck [S8].
 
-### [Data Compression and Storage Optimization](12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md)
-Reduces the memory footprint of 3DGS by compressing numerical representations, clustering parameters into codebooks, or replacing explicit attributes with neural fields [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md].
+### LS-Gaussian Viewpoint Transformation
+An algorithm that leverages inter-frame continuity for efficient sparse rendering by transforming the viewpoint between consecutive frames, rather than rendering each frame separately [S9]. It sets itself apart from naive pixel warping by operating at the tile/Gaussian level to reduce preprocessing and sorting costs [S9]. Choose this for real-time rendering on edge platforms (e.g., Jetson AGX Orin) where only one frame in every 6 frames is fully rendered [S9]. A pitfall is that rendering quality degrades as the number of consecutively transformed frames increases due to interpolation error accumulation [S9].
 
-### [Bézier-boundary gradient approximation](13137-b-zier-boundary-gradient-approximation.md)
-Approximates gradients of discontinuous indicator functions with respect to Bézier curve control points, enabling backpropagation in discontinuity-aware rendering [13137-b-zier-boundary-gradient-approximation.md].
